WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.491
I go to school in the world's biggest classroom.

2
00:00:04.492 --> 00:00:13.631
My classroom is more than double the size of the United Kingdom, more than one and a half million square kilometers.

3
00:00:13.632 --> 00:00:18.476
I only see my teacher and classmates a few times a year!

4
00:00:18.477 --> 00:00:21.922
I am a student at the School of the Air.

5
00:00:21.923 --> 00:00:30.219
The School of the Air is in Australia, and it teaches around one thousand students nationwide.

6
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:38.366
Many students, like me, live in isolated places in the vast area of Australia known as the outback.

7
00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:45.952
The outback is the huge desert-like area in the middle of Australia, away from its coasts.

8
00:00:45.953 --> 00:00:57.408
Very few people live here. My nearest classmate is a four-hour drive away, and the nearest town is a six-hour drive.

9
00:00:57.409 --> 00:01:03.041
I live at Pine Creek Cattle Station, Northern Territory, Australia.

10
00:01:03.042 --> 00:01:05.995
Outback kids are different from city kids—

11
00:01:05.996 --> 00:01:15.822
I am 12 years old, but I ride a horse, work on a farm, and drive a motorcycle. I don't have a license though!

12
00:01:15.823 --> 00:01:21.933
When I am 13, I will leave home and go to a boarding school in Darwin.

13
00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:27.708
Until then I will study at the School of the Air. How do I study?

14
00:01:27.709 --> 00:01:35.087
The word "air" is a clue—the School of the Air uses the radio.

15
00:01:35.088 --> 00:01:39.528
Monday to Friday I turn on the radio at eight o'clock.

16
00:01:39.529 --> 00:01:47.434
First up is the assembly. During assembly we sing the national anthem and the school song.

17
00:01:47.435 --> 00:01:57.632
Then we listen to other students do something special, like play the flute, read a poem they've written, or tell some jokes.

18
00:01:57.633 --> 00:02:02.170
After the assembly there is lesson time for two hours.

19
00:02:02.171 --> 00:02:08.324
Mom or Dad sits next to me during lessons and helps me with my homework.

20
00:02:08.325 --> 00:02:13.823
After lunch there are extra activities. I am in the choir.

21
00:02:13.824 --> 00:02:22.127
At first it's strange singing into a radio microphone by yourself, but you get used to it!

22
00:02:22.128 --> 00:02:28.644
On Wednesday I learn the flute over the radio, but Friday is my favorite day.

23
00:02:28.645 --> 00:02:39.532
After class is finished, I have a Girl Guides meeting—also on the radio! We are the Guides of the Air.

24
00:02:39.533 --> 00:02:42.932
But how do I study over the radio?

25
00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:51.383
First, I use a two-way radio, which means I can talk and listen to my teacher and the other students.

26
00:02:51.384 --> 00:03:01.278
Second, I use the Internet to email my teacher and look at websites for information and research.

27
00:03:01.279 --> 00:03:07.935
Third, every two weeks my teacher sends me books and homework through the mail.

28
00:03:07.936 --> 00:03:17.287
Fourth, I attend a school camp a few times a year, where I meet my teacher and my classmates face-to-face!

29
00:03:17.288 --> 00:03:24.485
Finally, my teacher comes and stays at my house for one week a year.

30
00:03:24.486 --> 00:03:29.086
Fifty years ago the School of the Air was very different.

31
00:03:29.087 --> 00:03:36.750
In those days the Flying Doctor was the only radio contact for people living in isolation.

32
00:03:36.751 --> 00:03:46.569
The Flying Doctor service is made up of doctors and nurses who fly to people in remote areas who need medical care.

33
00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:54.727
They give people medicine and medical advice or take them to the hospital.

34
00:03:54.728 --> 00:04:01.825
The Flying Doctor's radio, however, wasn't used just for medical news or advice.

35
00:04:01.826 --> 00:04:07.068
It also broadcast local news with its weekly galah session.

36
00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:19.379
A galah is an Australian bird that chatters a lot, and during the galah sessions, people talked to each other on the radio.

37
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.388
Adelaide Miethke was a teacher in Alice Springs.

38
00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:30.866
She listened to the galah sessions on the radio and recognized that outback kids were very shy

39
00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:35.117
because they didn't get to meet other children very often.

40
00:04:35.118 --> 00:04:41.517
Adelaide Miethke suggested to the government that they start the School of the Air.

41
00:04:41.518 --> 00:04:49.864
The first broadcast was from Alice Springs in 1951, using the Flying Doctor's radio.

42
00:04:49.865 --> 00:04:55.519
Now there are more than 16 Schools of the Air around Australia.

43
00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.641
Mom said that back then, the radio didn't sound so clear.

44
00:05:00.642 --> 00:05:04.350
There were often breaks and strange noises.

45
00:05:04.351 --> 00:05:10.052
The teacher even had to power the radio with her feet!

46
00:05:10.053 --> 00:05:18.075
Of course things have changed. The Internet and satellites mean that radio lessons may disappear altogether.

47
00:05:18.076 --> 00:05:26.479
In the future I will probably use an electronic whiteboard and receive my lessons via satellite.

48
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.231
In the meantime I enjoy my lessons over the airwaves.

49
00:05:31.232 --> 00:05:34.450
Over and out!

